Infinite Loops and Step Limits--

We are given a list of integer instructions and a step limit. The program execution starts at index 0. At each step, the instruction at the current index indicates the offset to jump (which may be positive or negative). The simulation stops when either:

The execution pointer goes out of bounds (which means the program terminates normally).
An instruction is revisited (indicating an infinite loop).
The number of steps executed reaches the specified step limit, in which case the simulation stops without a definite conclusion.
Constraints:

The list of instructions is nonempty and may have up to thousands of instructions.
Offsets can cause both forward and backward jumps.
A step limit is provided to prevent endless simulation on very long or infinite loops.
Section 2 — Intuition
The core idea is to simulate the program’s execution by moving an instruction pointer according to the current instruction’s offset. For the brute-force approach, we maintain a record of visited instruction indices so that if any instruction is executed a second time, we can detect an infinite loop. In detecting loop behavior without extra memory (optimal approach), we can borrow ideas from Floyd’s cycle-finding algorithm using slow and fast pointers. Both approaches must also check that the number of simulation steps does not exceed the given step limit.

Section 3 — Approach 1: Brute Force
In the brute-force solution, we use a Set (or an array of booleans) to record which indices have already been visited. We simulate each step as follows:

Check if the current instruction pointer is out of bounds (terminate if so).
Check if the current pointer has been visited before (if yes, declare an infinite loop).
Check the step limit; if reached, stop the simulation and report that the step limit has been exceeded.
If none of the above conditions are met, mark the index as visited, update the pointer based on the offset, and increment the step count.
The time complexity is O(n) in cases where the program terminates or a loop is quickly detected. The space complexity is O(n) due to tracking visited indices.

Copy
js
// Brute Force solution for detecting infinite loops and applying step limits
function simulateProgramBruteForce(instructions, stepLimit) {
  // current program pointer and step counter
  let pointer = 0;
  let steps = 0;
  // Set to track visited indices
  const visited = new Set();

  // Continue simulation until pointer is out of bounds
  while (pointer >= 0 && pointer < instructions.length) {
    if (visited.has(pointer)) {
      // Infinite loop detected – current instruction has been executed before.
      return {
        status: "infinite_loop",
        pointer,
        steps,
      };
    }
    if (steps >= stepLimit) {
      // Exceeded allowed simulation steps.
      return {
        status: "step_limit_exceeded",
        pointer,
        steps,
      };
    }
    // Mark the current pointer as visited.
    visited.add(pointer);
    // Get the jump offset from the current instruction.
    let offset = instructions[pointer];
    // Update pointer by applying the offset.
    pointer += offset;
    steps++;
  }

  // Terminated normally because pointer went out of bounds.
  return {
    status: "terminated",
    pointer,
    steps,
  };
}

// Example usage:
const instructions1 = [2, 3, 1, -3];
const stepLimit1 = 10;
console.log("Brute Force Result:", simulateProgramBruteForce(instructions1, stepLimit1));
Time Complexity: O(n) – each instruction is processed at most once before termination or detection.
Space Complexity: O(n) – maintaining a set of visited indices incurs extra space proportional to n.
Section 4 — Approach 2: Optimal Solution
An alternative optimal approach is to detect cycles using Floyd’s cycle detection algorithm (also known as the fast and slow pointer method). The idea is to advance two pointers: one (slow) that moves one step at a time and one (fast) that moves two steps per iteration. If there is a cycle, they will eventually meet. During the simulation, we also check for the step limit and normal termination. The method proceeds as follows:

Initialize two pointers (slow and fast) to the starting index (0) along with their corresponding step counts.
Advance the slow pointer by one step and the fast pointer by two steps (each using the simulation step logic).
If at any point a pointer goes out of bounds, then the simulation terminates normally.
If the pointers ever meet, a cycle (infinite loop) is detected.
Additionally, both pointers must check against the step limit at each move.
This approach uses constant extra space; however, one must carefully manage step counts alongside pointer updates.

Copy
js
// Optimal solution using Floyd's Cycle Detection for detecting infinite loops.
function simulateProgramOptimal(instructions, stepLimit) {
  let slow = 0;
  let fast = 0;
  let slowSteps = 0;
  let fastSteps = 0;

  // Helper function to take one step for a pointer.
  function takeStep(pointer, steps) {
    if (pointer < 0 || pointer >= instructions.length || steps >= stepLimit) {
      return { pointer, steps, terminated: true };
    }
    let next = pointer + instructions[pointer];
    return { pointer: next, steps: steps + 1, terminated: false };
  }

  while (true) {
    // Move slow pointer one step.
    const slowResult = takeStep(slow, slowSteps);
    slow = slowResult.pointer;
    slowSteps = slowResult.steps;
    if (slow < 0 || slow >= instructions.length || slowSteps >= stepLimit) {
      return {
        status: "terminated_or_step_limit",
        pointer: slow,
        steps: slowSteps,
      };
    }

    // Move fast pointer two steps.
    const fastResult1 = takeStep(fast, fastSteps);
    fast = fastResult1.pointer;
    fastSteps = fastResult1.steps;
    if (fast < 0 || fast >= instructions.length || fastSteps >= stepLimit) {
      return {
        status: "terminated_or_step_limit",
        pointer: fast,
        steps: fastSteps,
      };
    }
    const fastResult2 = takeStep(fast, fastSteps);
    fast = fastResult2.pointer;
    fastSteps = fastResult2.steps;
    if (fast < 0 || fast >= instructions.length || fastSteps >= stepLimit) {
      return {
        status: "terminated_or_step_limit",
        pointer: fast,
        steps: fastSteps,
      };
    }

    // If both pointers meet, an infinite loop is detected.
    if (slow === fast) {
      return {
        status: "infinite_loop_detected",
        pointer: slow,
        slowSteps,
        fastSteps,
      };
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
const instructions2 = [2, 3, 1, -3];
const stepLimit2 = 10;
console.log("Optimal Result:", simulateProgramOptimal(instructions2, stepLimit2));
Time Complexity: O(n) on average – the detection depends on meeting in the cycle and each pointer moves linearly with respect to steps.
Space Complexity: O(1) – only a constant amount of extra storage is used.
