Section 6 — Detailed Dry Run
Consider the following example:

Instructions: [2, 3, 1, -3]
Step limit: 10
Step	Pointer Before Move	Instruction Value	Pointer After Move	Action
0	0	2	0 + 2 = 2	Start simulation, mark index 0
1	2	1	2 + 1 = 3	Mark index 2 as visited
2	3	-3	3 + (-3) = 0	Mark index 3; pointer returns to 0
3	0	2	Already visited	Infinite loop detected (Brute Force)
...	...	...	...	If steps reached 10, would stop as step limit
For the optimal approach, the slow pointer would follow indices: 0 → 2 → 0 … while the fast pointer, moving twice as fast, would eventually meet the slow pointer, confirming the cycle.

Section 7 — Edge Cases
Empty instruction list → Although the problem defines a nonempty list, if encountered, the simulation should immediately terminate.
All instructions being the same value → Can either quickly exit or form a loop, depending on the offset.
List with a single element → May loop immediately if the instruction does not cause an out-of-bound jump.
Negative numbers leading to backward jumps → Proper pointer arithmetic handles these scenarios.
A program that terminates normally before reaching the step limit → The algorithm should correctly identify termination.
Section 8 — Common Mistakes
Not checking for out-of-bound pointers before accessing an instruction.
• Mistake: Directly using the pointer value without boundary check may lead to runtime errors.
Failing to update the step count before comparing with the step limit.
• Fix: Always increment and then check the step count.
Using an inappropriate data structure for visited indices (e.g., list not set) causing inefficient lookups.
• Fix: Use a Set for O(1) complexity on lookups.
In the optimal solution, neglecting to check the step limit for both fast and slow pointers.
• Fix: Ensure each pointer’s move is verified against the step limit.
Misinterpreting a loop detection condition by checking for equality after the move rather than before executing the next step.
• Fix: Clearly define the condition when an instruction is revisited.
